{"id":"int-0a393bee45782843657de6ca8d0e2415","kind":"field_change","created_at":"2026-08-09T16:41:12.944795Z","actor":"Justin Martin","issue_id":"syl-c1m","extra":{"field":"status","new_value":"in_progress","old_value":"open"}}
{"id":"int-a160702f37cc770dc3ab5606e880e78d","kind":"field_change","created_at":"2026-08-09T16:54:22.137845Z","actor":"Justin Martin","issue_id":"syl-qnf","extra":{"field":"status","new_value":"closed","old_value":"open","reason":"Root cause was not session.ts at all: the process-level tests spawned the entry point through tsx, a wrapper, so kill() signalled the wrapper and exitCode reported the wrapper's status. Under load the second SIGTERM killed tsx while it waited on a child still shutting down. Now runs the built dist under process.execPath. Also raised vitest's 5s default timeout, which is not a valid assumption for a suite whose job is spawning subprocesses. Four consecutive full-suite runs green."}}
{"id":"int-497e402d9b903d45a2135a647f1db4ee","kind":"field_change","created_at":"2026-08-09T17:06:07.827972Z","actor":"Justin Martin","issue_id":"syl-114","extra":{"field":"status","new_value":"closed","old_value":"open","reason":"Fixed by scripts/check-workspace-deps.mjs, modelled on the check-node.mjs precedent the bead itself recommended. Wired with check-node into a single check:env hook before test, typecheck and build. Proven to fire: in a real fresh worktree it names the three unresolvable workspaces and exits 1; in an installed checkout it is silent. Turns a silent collection failure that reads as absence into one loud line naming the cause and the fix."}}
{"id":"int-e17ea95229e5196d6dbc06a3b5d58c72","kind":"field_change","created_at":"2026-08-09T17:07:32.823938Z","actor":"Justin Martin","issue_id":"syl-iwb","extra":{"field":"status","new_value":"closed","old_value":"open","reason":"Verified rather than assumed — checked each of the bead's three claims separately.\n\n1. 'main() registers no SIGINT/SIGTERM handler'. It does now: installShutdownHandlers runs in main(), and as of today it runs BEFORE startSyl rather than after, so there is no window where the service answers health checks while a SIGTERM would still kill it outright.\n\n2. 'stop/close/close are called only from tests'. The shutdown path now calls runtime.stop(), service.close() and database.close() (index.ts:600-601, 655).\n\n3. THE ONE THAT ACTUALLY MATTERED — 'nothing releases the lease on the way down, so a restart stalls for the full lease window'. runtime.stop() disarms the runner and then DRAINS: it waits for the pass already in the air to finish, so the in-flight job completes and releases its lease through the normal path instead of being abandoned. A restart resumes immediately rather than waiting out the TTL, which is what the bead was really asking for.\n\nProof file named in the bead, backend/tests/acceptance/us5-she-survives-a-restart.test.ts, passes 11/11.\n\nHardened twice more today while chasing an unrelated flake: the handlers are no longer removed before the process actually exits (a SIGTERM in that window killed it mid-write, status 143), and a repeated SIGINT now forces an exit while a repeated SIGTERM stays swallowed, because launchd re-sends SIGTERM as a matter of course."}}
{"id":"int-5dcda3a969d7e0266aefb5a1762abaf5","kind":"field_change","created_at":"2026-08-09T17:16:38.132772Z","actor":"Justin Martin","issue_id":"syl-q1f","extra":{"field":"status","new_value":"in_progress","old_value":"open"}}
{"id":"int-f668766e43d8cd498f796f7c35cd5b65","kind":"field_change","created_at":"2026-08-09T17:16:56.094146Z","actor":"Justin Martin","issue_id":"syl-q1f","extra":{"field":"status","new_value":"closed","old_value":"in_progress","reason":"Pairing exists end to end and is proven at every seam.\n\nBACKEND\n- 0009_pairing_codes.sql: codes live in the store, hashed with scrypt over a\n  per-row salt (N=16384, ~40ms). On disk because 'npm run pair' is a different\n  process from the launchd service and the database file is all they share; and\n  scrypt rather than SHA-256 because eight digits under SHA-256 is reversible in\n  seconds by anyone who can read syl.db. The 40ms doubles as the rate limit on\n  the one unauthenticated write.\n- SINGLE USE IS AT THE STORE: api_keys.pairing_code_id UNIQUE, plus a conditional\n  UPDATE ... WHERE redeemed_at IS NULL in the same transaction as the key insert.\n  One code mints at most one key regardless of what calling code does.\n- issuePairingCode() now supersedes rather than memoises: exactly one live code\n  at any instant.\n- npm run pair: prints the code, the expiry, and the tailnet URL read from the\n  certificate's own hostname (null rather than a guess when unknown).\n- Startup names 'npm run pair' on every boot, which is what made the flow\n  discoverable at all after the first device.\n\nCONTRACT\nPAIRING_CODE_EXPIRED and PAIRING_CODE_ALREADY_USED added to ErrorCode, with\nfixtures, regenerated types, Swift cases, and an honest mock that publishes\ncodes so all four outcomes are reachable without a Mac. Safe to distinguish\nbecause both are reachable ONLY by presenting a code that matches a stored one;\na wrong guess and 'no window open' stay one indistinguishable UNAUTHORIZED.\n\niOS\nFeatures/Pairing (view + view model), gated on AppDelegate.isPaired. Token to\nthe Keychain as AfterFirstUnlockThisDeviceOnly, never UserDefaults, never\nsynchronizable. whoami on launch tells a dead token from a tunnel still coming\nup. Idempotency key derived (SHA-256), not generated — a fresh key per attempt\nreintroduces syl-ux1 on the one write with no recovery path. ServerProfile.from\naccepts every shape of the right answer.\n\nTESTED\nbackend 2099 pass across three consecutive full 'npm run verify' runs; new\ncoverage includes two connections to one real migrated SQLite file (:memory:\nwould make those pass by not testing anything), the four route failure states,\nand the whole flow through startSyl over a real socket.\nTHE SWIFT ACTUALLY COMPILED AND RAN: ios/scripts/test.sh exits 0 across all\nthree phases — swift test (210), the live-backend phase (LiveServerTests now\ndrives SylAPI.pair at a real service with a real code), and xcodebuild test on\nthe simulator (143, of which 22 are new)."}}
